> What is the weird code displayed in the message above?

It is called Rot-13, for "rotate 13 places right" after its
common Net implementation in the minimal 26 letter, two case
English alphabet.

> I managed to decode the message after about half an hour
> of work, but I'm still curious.

Well done! It's formal name is a "transposition cypher", by
the way, but even among them it is simple because the
transpositions are regular rather than stirred.

> Is this a well-known code, or something invented by
> Nethack fans?

This is well known code. Some newspapers publish a regular
short piece of transposition cypher to be decoded as an
amusement, regularly. It is ridiculous as "security" today,
but it is still quite successful at baffling the eye.  After
working with it long enough, though, it is just something
else you learn how to read. Nethack fans did not invent it,
nor very likely was its first use among computer users even
Usenet News, but it has been around in automated form since
"rogue" was "scamp", at the very least.

> Who invented it?

It was invented, we are told, by Julius Caeser as a way to
encrypt his war dispatches, so is also called "Caeser Code"
[though since the original Latin alphabet wasn't 26
characters long, and (I think) was monocased, the rotation
amount was unlikely then to have been precisely 13].

> Are there automatic computer programs out there that can
> decode these things, or do you have to do it by hand every
> time?

Some news reading software will rotate an article body with
a couple of keystrokes, which is quite nice.  For me it is
slower, since I read with a browser, not "newsreading
software", so I cut and paste the messages into the vim()
editor, which, again, has a built in, brief-to-type ROT-13
capability for any selected region of text.

Probably the "simplest" stand alone program to translate this
kind of code is the Unix tr() program, where something
possibly spelled

  tr "a-zA-Z" "n-zA-Za-m" < encryptedForm > decryptedForm

suffices.

